BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cashflow Projections: The Numbers Your Business Needs to Work
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6a99bacfe1fb8f13578bc547@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This program is part of the Toronto Reference Library's\nEntre
 preneur in Residence\n(EIR) service.\nThis session will teach you how to a
 ssess your numbers to determine if your business will survive. We will cov
 er:\nCosts of operation\nWhat      each sale earns after costs\nHow many  
     sales it takes to break even\nBuilding      a straightforward monthly 
 cash flow projection\nSetting justifiable prices\nWe'll work through the n
 umbers of real businesses together before you begin thinking about your ow
 n. No accounting experience required — bring a calculator or your phone.
 \nThe program includes a short networking component\, providing participan
 ts with an opportunity to connect with other entrepreneurs.\nRegistration 
 recommended. Drop-ins welcome until room capacity is reached.\nTo apply fo
 r one-on-one consultations with our resident entrepreneur\, Ryan\, please 
 fill out the in-take form available at\ntpl.ca/eir\n. We begin accepting i
 n-take forms on September 28\, 2026 until available slots are filled.\nInf
 ormation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is coll
 ected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, secti
 ons 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event
  and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how t
 he library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at
 \nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2
 G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, Ground Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a99bacfe1fb8f13578bc547
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Three “Know’s for Success as an Entrepreneur
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6aaad5e3ab7a8e0037c24145@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This program is part of Fort York Library's\nEntrepreneur in R
 esidence\n(EIR) service\nIn this highly interactive workshop with Entrepre
 neur in Residence (Newcomer Focus)\, Jean Chow\, we’ll explore how well 
 you know yourself\, know your reason why you want to own a business\, and 
 will your customers need and want to buy what you are offering. What are t
 he key traits of an entrepreneur? Is this an opportunity worth pursuing\, 
 a T.R.U.E. (Testable\, Real. Unique\, Essential) value proposition? Who ar
 e your customers?\nThis session will include:\nKnow your dominant dimensio
 n as an entrepreneur – “heart\, smarts\, guts\, or luck”.\nPinpoint 
 your “why” with your T.R.U.E. value proposition and articulate the pro
 blem you are solving. Is it an opportunity worth pursuing?\nCreate a buyer
  persona/ideal customer profile (ICP) to demonstrate how well you know you
 r target audience.\nCome early to network from 6-6:30pm!\nTo apply for one
 -on-one consultations with our resident entrepreneur\, Jean\, please fill 
 out the in-take form available at\ntpl.ca/eir\n.\nInformation Collection N
 otice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto 
 Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, a
 nd 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registra
 tion\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles yo
 ur information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n
 \, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Fort York\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aaad5e3ab7a8e0037c24145
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Lede: A Live Editorial Meeting Experience
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6a85b8bdf564ef0d72c0eb8c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL and the Canadian Journalism Foundation present a special M
 edia Literacy week event!\nThree journalists. One front page. Only one sto
 ry leads.\nThe Lede pulls back the curtain on a process most people never 
 see: how editors weigh impact\, timeliness\, public interest and audience 
 relevance under pressure\, in real time.\nThree journalists from The Globe
  and Mail\, Canada's national newspaper\, deputy head of investigations Ro
 byn Doolittle\, politics and economy editor Ryan MacDonald and senior visu
 als editor Solana Cain\, will each pitch the story they believe deserves t
 he lead position in tomorrow's news. Editor-in-chief David Walmsley will q
 uestion their assumptions\, challenge their reasoning and make the final c
 all\, live\, in front of the audience.\nThe audience does not just watch. 
 Before Walmsley announces his decision\, they vote on which story should l
 ead\, then see how their judgment compares with his.\nJoin us for a chance
  to understand not just what the news is\, but how it gets made\, and to w
 alk away a sharper\, more critical reader of it.\nAbout this event’s gue
 sts:\nDavid Walmsley\nis\nThe Globe and Mail\n’s editor-in-chief and the
  current president of the World Editors Forum. He is the co-creator of Wor
 ld News Day\, a global day of education involving more than 1\,000 publish
 ers across 100 countries explaining the role of journalism and how it can 
 make the world a better place. Under his editorship\, The Globe has won th
 e annual Canadian Michener Award for meritorious public service journalism
  five times and has dominated the annual National Newspaper Awards.\nRobyn
  Doolittle\nis the deputy head of investigations at\nThe Globe and Mail\n.
  Doolittle came to\nThe Globe\nin 2014 to join the investigative unit. Aft
 er a decade on the i-Team\, she spent time in the Report on Business cover
 ing corporate law. She has investigated political and business corruption\
 , a series of shootings and arsons targeting Canadian waste giant GFL\, th
 e Hockey Canada scandal\, gender inequities in the workforce and Canada’
 s broken freedom of information system as part of the Secret Canada projec
 t. Her two books\, “Had It Coming – What’s Fair In The Age of #MeToo
 ?\,” and “Crazy Town: The Rob Ford Story\,” were both national bests
 ellers.\nRyan MacDonald\nis the editor of politics and economy at\nThe Glo
 be and Mail\n\, leading a talented team of reporters covering the intersec
 tion of business and economic policy\, including energy\, finance\, trade 
 and fiscal matters. Since joining\nThe Globe\nin 2007\, MacDonald has held
  multiple editorial positions and was notably part of the team that won th
 e 2022 CJF Climate Solutions Reporting Award for its narrative-shifting st
 ories on how to re-engineer Canada’s economy to adapt to and capitalize 
 on climate change. Prior to joining\nThe Globe\n\, MacDonald served as the
  national editor at the\nOttawa Citizen\n.\nSolana Cain\nis a senior visua
 ls editor at\nThe Globe and Mail\n\, Canada’s national newspaper. She jo
 ined\nThe Globe\nthrough the 2019 summer program\, quickly distinguishing 
 herself as a perceptive and composed photo editor. In 2020\, Cain helped e
 stablish Room Up Front\, a mentorship initiative supporting BIPOC photojou
 rnalists. The program received a special citation at the 2023 National New
 spaper Awards for its significant contribution to Canadian journalism. A d
 edicated mentor and educator\, she actively advises emerging photographers
  and lectures at Toronto Metropolitan University\, Sheridan College\, and 
 Loyalist College.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel 
 Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nD
 oors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration requi
 red.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest
  number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 
 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nB
 ar\n: Beer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\nAccessibility
 :\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability
  and would like to request accommodation for this event\, please contact a
 ccessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the
  event.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nOn Civil Society\nserie
 s\, where we host local and international authors\, activists and academic
 s in conversation about the most critical topics of our time. Generously s
 upported by the McConnell Foundation and also by Chris M. Reid.\n***\nInfo
 rmation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is colle
 cted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sectio
 ns 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event 
 and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be con
 tacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Questions about h
 ow the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staf
 f at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 
 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b8bdf564ef0d72c0eb8c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Seagoing Women: Author Sara Donnelly & Panel Talk with Water Women
  At Work
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6aac12164b3b060030846a41@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Journalist\, mariner\, and author of\nSeagoing Women: At Work\
 n\,\nSara Donnelly\n\, creates captivating portraits of thirty-three women
  who have built remarkable careers on the water\, everything from tugboat 
 operators and commercial fishers to naval architects and whale researchers
 .\nThrough vivid first-person accounts\, this panel talk will offer a rare
  glimpse at offshore life: the physical demands\, long stretches away from
  home\, ingenuity required in unpredictable conditions and deep sense of p
 urpose shared by those whose lives are tied to the ocean.\nModerated by Do
 nnelly\, this panel talk will feature:\nOcean Rutherford\n- West Coast Can
 adian mariner\, tugboat deckhand\, commercial fisher\, and children's book
  author from Prince Rupert\, British Columbia. Ocean Rutherford made headl
 ines in December 2023 as part of the first all-female tugboat crew in SAAM
  Towage Canada's 70-year history.\nWeyla Chipp\ns - After raising four ch
 ildren and working as an education assistant in the Sooke School District\
 , Weyla Chipps of the Scia’new First Nation seized the chance to train f
 or a new life at sea: working on anchor handlers in the North Sea and emba
 rking on adventures through the Bermuda Triangle and along the Panama Cana
 l.\nConnie Bell-Irving -\nOriginally from Australia\, Connie Bell-Irving b
 ecame the first woman on the BC coast to command oil barges and later the 
 first in Canada to receive Transport Canada's full endorsement as Supervis
 or of an Oil Transfer Operation. Today she is part of HaiSea Marine in Kit
 imat\, the world's first partnership of its kind between the Haisla Nation
  and Seaspan\, operating the most sustainable tug fleet in the industry.\n
 Join in to be inspired by the stories and career experiences that highligh
 ts determination\, skill and resilience in a profession defined by adventu
 re\, challenge and reward.\nPresented as part of Women's History Month. Pl
 ease note that the program time is\n7:00 to 8:00 pm Eastern Time (Toronto)
 .\nRegistration:\nThis virtual event is free to join and will take place o
 n\nZoom\n.\nRegister to receive an email with details on how to join the 
 session and to get a link to the recording.\nCaptions will be available.\n
 Need help with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and participating in a Zoom webinar\n-\nZ
 oom Privacy Statement\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Library:
 \nSeagoing Women : At Work\n(book)\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe pe
 rsonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library
  under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The 
 information will only be used for event and program registration\, deliver
 y and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information
  can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-598
 1\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aac12164b3b060030846a41
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google AI Essentials: Online Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6aa46e06ab7a8e0037c12494@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Curious about artificial intelligence or want to learn practic
 al AI skills for work and personal use? Join our staff-facilitated learnin
 g circle to complete the self-paced Google AI Essentials specialization.\n
 This 10-hour introductory-level specialization\, offered through Coursera\
 , is available for free to learning circle participants. It is made up of 
 five courses\, each containing four units that consist of short videos\, a
 rticles\, assignments and a short multiple-choice quiz.\nBy completing the
  specialization\,\nyou will learn:\nHow AI works\nHow to write effective p
 rompts\nHow to use generative AI tools responsibly\nHow to identify AI's p
 otential biases and avoid harm\nHow to stay up to date with AI news\nAfter
  you complete the course\, you earn a Google certificate and a badge to sh
 are with others\nLearning Circles are study groups guided by\nlibrary staf
 f for people who want to complete the Google AI Essentials specialization 
 together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work together thr
 ough course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit to attending a
 ll four learning circle sessions.\nNo AI experience is required.\nHow to J
 oi\nn a Learning Circle:\nRegister once on the first session of this event
  series\, in order to attend all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a\nvalid e
 mail address\n.\nA valid TPL library card is required for online circles
 .\nAttend all four sessions online via Zoom\, Tuesday October 6\, 13\, 20 
 and 27 from 6-8 PM.\nRegistration is a two-step procedure. First\, partici
 pants must complete their registration through this page. After this step\
 , library staff will follow up to request additional information in order 
 to complete the registration.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe persona
 l information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library unde
 r the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The infor
 mation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and
  evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can 
 be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 7
 89 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa46e06ab7a8e0037c12494
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
