BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Data Analytics Essentials Online Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T193000
UID:6a6a661db57350d760b31f42@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Interested in learning about data analytics? Let's take a free
  online course together in a welcoming space!\nA\nlearning circle\nis a st
 udy group\, guided by library staff\, for people who want to take a free o
 nline course together in a supportive and welcoming environment.\nOrganiza
 tions all over the world recognize the value of analytics. Together\, we'l
 l learn about the essential tools used in this field.\nIn this 12-week lea
 rning circle\, we will work through the Data Analytics Essentials course f
 rom Cisco Networking Academy. We will explore the fundamental tools of a d
 ata analyst\, including how to:\ntransform\, organize and visualize data w
 ith spreadsheet tools like Excel\nquery data from a relational database us
 ing SQL\nimprove data presentations using business intelligence tools like
  Tableau\nWe will meet online every Thursday evening\,\nSeptember 24 - De
 cember 10\, 6-7:30 PM\n(12 sessions).\nParticipants are expected to commit
  to the duration of the course and attend sessions on a weekly basis.\nLea
 rners who complete the course will be able to:\nearn a digital badge\ndown
 load a certificate of completion
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6a661db57350d760b31f42
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Colour & Atmosphere
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T200000
UID:6ab172ae4ea230006f34ddf8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an online special career series with the\n2026 Joa
 nne Fitzgerald Illustrator in Residence\n\, Elly MacKay! In this series\,
  Elly will share insights into building a career in children’s book ill
 ustration\, covering topics such as the journey to working as an illustrat
 or\, an overview of classic children’s books\, portfolios\, and colour. 
 Details about each session follow:\nThursday\, October 8\, 7pm:\nBecoming 
 an Illustrator\nElly MacKay shares her early and current work\, her career
  path\, and her process for creating miniature 3D worlds. Her talk also co
 vers developing your style\, illustration trends\, and how to stand out.\n
 Thursday\, October 15\, 7pm: Looking at Picture Books\nElly MacKay gives 
 an overview of famous picture books\, the various styles\, what works\, an
 d how they’ve changed over the years. She looks at cover design\, struct
 ure\, page turns\, and how visual storytelling and words work together.\nT
 hursday\, October 22\, 7pm: From Portfolio to Book\nElly MacKay discusses
  portfolios and what to include\, as well as working with agents and publi
 shers. She also looks at what goes into creating your first picture book.\
 nThursday\, October 29\, 7pm: Colour and Atmosphere\nElly MacKay conside
 rs colour in picture books—how it can be used to strengthen a message\, 
 or to alter the mood. She looks at how illustrators have used colour in un
 conventional ways in their books.\nWorkshops will be recorded and availabl
 e for replay through Zoom until October 31\, 2026.\nAbout Elly MacKay\nEll
 y MacKay is a children’s book author and illustrator and a paper artist.
  She studied printmaking and illustration at NSCAD University in Halifax a
 nd University of Canterbury in Christchurch. Elly creates miniature worlds
  with drawing\, painting and cutting\, and photographs them for illustrati
 ons in children’s books. She has more than 20 books published in Canada 
 and the US\, including The Enchanted Symphony by Julie Andrews and Emma Wa
 lton Hamilton\, I Am a Meadow Mermaid and The Secret Fawn by Kallie George
 . Elly is both author and illustrator of Frostfire\, In the Clouds and Red
  Sky at Night and others. She lives with her family in Owen Sound\, Ontari
 o.\nAbout the Joanne Fitzgerald Illustrator in Residence Program\nThe\nIll
 ustrator in Residence\nprogram honours the memory of illustrator Joanne Fi
 tzgerald (1956-2011)\, and is a joint program of IBBY Canada\, Toronto Pub
 lic Library\, and the Young Family.\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to a
 ccessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to reque
 st accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca
  or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\nInformation Col
 lection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the
  Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 
 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program
  registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library h
 andles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline
 @tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab172ae4ea230006f34ddf8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jesse Wente: Tough Lies
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T200000
UID:6a85b674fa71a30d92946b59@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Award-winning author Jesse Wente shares his highly-anticipated
  new book exploring how we can heal from colonialism.\nIn Tough Lies\, Wen
 te explores his own healing path and how it informs what he sees as necess
 ary for reconciliation between Nations\, asking us to consider several pre
 ssing questions.\nWhat does healing from colonialism look like for First N
 ations? What about non-Indigenous people? What are the barriers to this he
 aling\, and what can we do to move past them?\nThrough a lens of healing a
 nd anti-colonial thought\, Wente examines his reaction to incidents that h
 ave hit close to home\, chronicling his personal journey and what it has t
 aught him about the present and future of reconciliation in Canada.\nJesse
  Wente speaks with professor Kim Anderson about Tough Lies and the path fo
 r healing between Nations.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books availabl
 e for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nJesse Wente\nKim Anderson\n
 Read more:\nTough Lies : Indigenous Belonging and My Healing Through Golf\
 nUnreconciled\nLife Stages and Native Women\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocati
 on:\nConcourse Event Space\, North York Central Library (5120 Yonge St)\nT
 ime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registratio
 n required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the g
 reatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed
  until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first ser
 ved.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we 
 host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversatio
 n about their new books and big ideas.\nIt is also part of our\nIndigenous
  Heritage series\n\, generously supported by TD Bank Group\, through the T
 D Ready Commitment.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal inf
 ormation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the
  Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informatio
 n will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and eval
 uation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following 
 the program. Questions about how the library handles your information can 
 be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 
 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b674fa71a30d92946b59
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
