BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google Prompting Essentials: Online Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T203000
UID:6aa464e586d8450031156f91@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Ready to develop the skills to use generative AI (GenAI) tools
  effectively and responsibly for work and daily life? Join our staff-facil
 itated Learning Circle to complete the self-paced Google Prompting special
 ization. By completing this Learning Circle program\, you will earn a Goo
 gle Prompting Essentials certificate to share on your resume\, LinkedIn an
 d social media profiles!\nWhat You'll Learn\nThis program is designed for 
 those with some experience with using GenAI. Through short videos\, articl
 es\, assignments and quizzes\, you will discover:\nEssential\nPrompting Sk
 ills\n: How to use a prompting framework to create a desired output with G
 enAI\; how to evaluate and refine your results.\nEveryday Work Tasks:\nStr
 ategies to draft content\, brainstorm ideas\, create tables\, timelines\, 
 and more.\nData & Presentations:\nHow to explore data visualization and us
 e GenAI responsibly when working with data.\nAdvanced Techniques:\nHow to 
 use prompt chaining for complex tasks and prompt versioning to save and re
 use your best prompts over time.\nLearning Circles are study groups guided
  by\nlibrary staff for people who want to complete the Google Prompting sp
 ecialization together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work
  together through course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit t
 o attending all four Learning Circle sessions.\nSome Experience with GenAI
  Recommended: This course is ideal for participants who have completed the
  AI Essentials course Learning Circle.\nHow to Joi\nn a Learning Circle:\n
 Register once for the first session of this event series\, in order to att
 end all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a valid email address.\nA valid TPL
  library card is required for online circles.\nAttend all four sessions 
 online via Zoom\,\nThursdays Oct. 8\, 15\, 22 and 29 from 6-8:30pm.\nRegis
 tration is a two-step process. First\, participants must complete their re
 gistration through this page. After this step\, library staff will follow 
 up to request additional information in order to complete the registration
 .\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form i
 s collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\,
  sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for
  event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about
  how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline st
 aff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON
  M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa464e586d8450031156f91
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: The Fragile Wild — Photographs by Roshan Pan
 jwani
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260901T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T235959
UID:6a875a463b6c71003e5a39be@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of September and October\, the works of p
 hotographer Roshan Panjwani are on display at\nDeer Park branch\n.\nRosh
 an Panjwani is a Toronto-based nature and wildlife photographer\, original
 ly from India. Living with a physical disability has meant approaching fie
 ldwork differently and has reinforced the persistence the work demands. Ov
 er more than a decade he has photographed across a wide range of habitats:
  the tiger reserves of India\, the Okavango Delta and salt pans of Botswan
 a\, the savannahs of East Africa\, the Himalayan high desert of Ladakh\, t
 he rainforests of Costa Rica\, the mountains and coast of Alaska and the C
 anadian Rockies\, and the High Arctic fjords of Greenland and Svalbard. Hi
 s photographs have appeared on the cover of Canadian Photography magazine\
 , been featured by BBC Earth\, and appear in Europe's Sea Mammals\, publis
 hed by Princeton University Press. Through his work he hopes to deepen app
 reciation and care for the planet's fragile ecosystems and the wildlife in
 habiting them.\n"I photograph nature and wildlife in some of the world's l
 ess-explored places — pristine habitats\, distinctive species\, and land
 scapes that can feel otherworldly. Much of my work entails long hours in t
 he field\, watching for the light to shift or for wildlife behaviour that 
 makes the frame memorable."\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Sp
 ace\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to e
 xhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to refl
 ect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nTh
 e exhibit can be viewed during Deer Park's open hours. Please check the\nD
 eer Park branch\npage before your visit. The branch will be closed on Sep
 tember 6th and 7th.\nFree. All are welcome.\nImage credit\nPhotographer: R
 oshan Panjwani\nTitle of Piece: King of Ice\nMedium: Fine art pigment pri
 nt\, face-mounted to acrylic\nSize/Dimensions: 30" x 45"
LOCATION:Deer Park\, Main Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a875a463b6c71003e5a39be
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
