BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:String Secrets:  Violin Scams and Fraud
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T200000
UID:6a73bd03cca66c2f00a88469@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Art crimes have always captured public attention\, but those i
 n the musical world are generally less known.\nJoin us as professional vio
 linist and music historian Susan Spier (MA) gives a presentation on art cr
 imes as related to violins: from simple performance scams\, to counterfeit
  strings\, fake labels\, and instrument fraud.\nAlso covered will be true 
 stories of famous stolen Strads\, exploring the current status of specific
  instruments (if known) and discussing what is being done to prevent such 
 crimes in the future.\nDrop in. No registration required. For more informa
 tion\, please contact the Arts Department at 416-393-7157.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall (ground level)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a73bd03cca66c2f00a88469
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jennifer Botterill & Cassie Campbell-Pascall: Game Changers
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T200000
UID:6a85b657e90c830d873fe3b2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Trailblazing hockey players Jennifer Botterill and Cassie Camp
 bell-Pascall share their new memoirs.\nBotterill broke barriers as the you
 ngest player on Team Canada at the first Olympics for women’s ice hockey
 \, while Campbell-Pascall set records as the longest-serving captain in Ca
 nadian hockey history—and the first of any gender to lead the country to
  two Olympic gold medals.\nSince their Hall of Fame playing careers\, both
  have remained at the very top their game as sought-after broadcasters and
  advocates for athletes. Now\, in their memoirs Why Not You and Just a G
 irl\, these two titans of hockey invite us into their worlds like never be
 fore.\nJennifer Botterill and Cassie Campbell-Pascall speak with sportscas
 ter Ailish Forfar about their new memoirs\, taking a look back on their de
 corated hockey careers and toward a brighter future for all who play the g
 ame.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout
  this event’s guests:\nJennifer Botterill\nCassie Campbell-Pascall\nAili
 sh Forfar\nRead more:\nWhy Not You : My Story of Perseverance\, Hockey\, a
 nd Olympic Gold\nJust A Girl : A Memoir\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\
 nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)
 \, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nF
 ree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to en
 sure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tic
 kets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first c
 ome\, first served.\nBar:\nBeer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit 
 only).\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where w
 e host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversat
 ion about their new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Noti
 ce:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Pub
 lic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 
 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registratio
 n\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a br
 ief survey following the program. Questions about how the library handles 
 your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\
 , 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b657e90c830d873fe3b2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bee-ing a Pollinator Protector: Plan Now for a Buzzing Spring Gard
 en
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T200000
UID:6abc0fdb377296003a4874e7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Saving the bees is an environmental cause that resonates deepl
 y with Canadians.  While most of us might not be thinking about bees this 
 time of year\, our actions during the colder months can still support thes
 e pollinators\, particularly native bees\, which are crucial and threatene
 d by climate change\, habitat loss\, and other pressures.\nIn honour of th
 e late bee scientist Sheila Colla\, this presentation will offer practical
  tips on planting and maintaining gardens that provide beautifully bloomin
 g habitat for pollinators\, with a particular focus on how native plants s
 upport wild bees.\nPresented by\nLorraine Johnson\n\, who co-authored\nA G
 arden for the Rusty-Patched Bumblebee\nwith Sheila Colla\, as part of TPL'
 s environmental program series\,\nOur Fragile Planet\n\, supported by Frie
 nds of Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.​\nThis program is also pr
 esented as part of Climate Action Week.\nRegistration:\nThis virtual even
 t is free to join and will take place on\nZoom\n.\nRegister to receive an 
 email with details on how to join the session. Join live\, as there will n
 ot be a recording for this event.\nNeed help with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and pa
 rticipating in a Zoom webinar\n-\nZoom Privacy Statement\nPlace a hold or 
 borrow from Toronto Public Library:\nA Garden for the Rusty-patched Bumble
 bee\n(book)\nA Garden for the Rusty-Patched Bumblebee\n(ebook)\n---\nThis 
 program will also be offered in person on Monday\, November 23 at the Riv
 erdale Branch. To register for the online program and for more info\, see 
 the listing [COMING SOON].\n---\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe perso
 nal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library un
 der the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The inf
 ormation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery a
 nd evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information ca
 n be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\,
  789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abc0fdb377296003a4874e7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
