BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Doubles: A Panel Talk About the Film\, Diversity & Indo-Caribbean 
 Voices
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261107T160000
UID:6ad78fc3e06996003a84bc1e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an extraordinary evening with the actors and produ
 ction team behind the six-time\, award-nominated film\nDoubles\n\, where d
 iversity meets imagination\, identity meets history and rarely heard Indo-
 Caribbean voices come together to celebrate the power of storytelling.\nTh
 e Canadian drama film\, Doubles\, set in both Trinidad and Toronto\, explo
 res the relationship between a son and his estranged father. When tragedy 
 strikes\, Dhani\, a Doubles stand operator dreaming of owning his own rest
 aurant\, must leave Trinidad to find his father\, whom he hasn't seen in y
 ears. Their reunion brings deeply held feelings to the surface as unexpect
 ed revelations change their lives forever.\nPresented by the\nCanadian Car
 ibbean Indian Cultural Collective (CCICC)\n\, this panel will feature:\nMa
 rk Sirju\n\, Producer\nErrol Sitahal\n\, Actor portraying Ragbir\nPremika 
 Leo\n\, Actor portraying Skye\nKenneth Mohabir\n\, comedian\, actor\, and 
 producer\, will moderate the discussion.\nEnjoy a discussion about the imp
 ortance of Indo-Caribbean representation\, behind-the-scenes stories about
  the film\, and watch selected scenes from the film.  An audience Q&A will
  follow the panel presentation.\nFree program. Please register. Doors open
  at 1:45 pm. At 2:00 pm\, seating becomes first-come\, first-served until 
 capacity is reached.\nOrganization Info:\nThe\nCanadian Caribbean Indian C
 ultural Collective (CCICC)\nis a community organization representing India
 ns (currently referred to as South Asians) of various ethnicities\, region
 s\, and faiths\, who are the descendants of the indentured labourers taken
  by the British to their colonies in the Caribbean and around the world. T
 heir mission is to share their stories of struggle\, resilience and contri
 butions across the Caribbean and here in Canada.\nInformation Collection N
 otice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto 
 Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, a
 nd 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registra
 tion\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles yo
 ur information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n
 \, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Theatre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad78fc3e06996003a84bc1e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
