BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Changing the Narrative: Toronto’s Indigenous – Settler History
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261109T200000
UID:6a8dad66aafa6100295ef816@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn more about Toronto's multifaceted Indigenous history\, w
 hich has often been ignored or minimized due to the telling of the region'
 s history only from settler perspectives. Join us for a panel discussion a
 bout a public history project that worked with five local First Nations to
  create a more accurate and inclusive story of the region's past\, and is
  transforming the way settlement of 19th century Ontario is represented a
 t The Village at Black Creek (formerly Black Creek Pioneer Village).\nChan
 ging the Narrative: Reconnecting Settler and Indigenous Histories at Black
  Creek Pioneer Village was a collaborative project of York University hist
 orians\, The Village at Black Creek\, Jumblies Theatre\, and the Mississau
 gas of the Credit\, Chippewas of Rama\, Six Nations of the Grand River\, C
 hippewas of Georgina Island\, and Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nati
 ons.\nThis panel discussion will share research highlights\, reflect on th
 e process of collaboration\, and explore how the project is expanding and 
 enriching storytelling at one of the region’s prime living history museu
 ms.\nThe project’s findings have also been published in Where Histories 
 Meet: Indigenous and Settler Encounters in the Toronto Area\, by Victoria 
 Freeman. The book explores this history from multiple perspectives in an a
 ccessible and richly illustrated format and includes excerpts from intervi
 ews with twenty Indigenous elders and knowledge keepers. It was nominated
  for a\n2026 City of Toronto Book Award\nand is the winner of the\n2026 H
 eritage Toronto Book Award.\nPanel members include:\nVictoria      Freeman
 \n\, historical researcher and author of\nWhere Histories Meet:      Indig
 enous and Settler Encounters in the Toronto Area\n.\nJennifer      Bonnell
 \n\, professor in the Department of History at York University and the    
   lead of the Changing the Narrative Project.\nAllison      White\n\, cura
 tor at The Village at Black Creek (Toronto and Region      Conservation Au
 thority).\nDarin      Wybenga\n\, heritage interpreter for the Mississauga
 s of the Credit First      Nation\, and member of the Research Committee f
 or the Changing the      Narrative project.\nLauri      Hoeg\n\, Anishinaa
 be artist and cultural advisor from the Chippewas of      Georgina Island 
 First Nation\, and member of the Research Committee for the      Changing 
 the Narrative project.\nThe panel discussion will last approximately 60 mi
 nutes\, followed by a Q&A with audience members. No registration required.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, 1st Floor\, Beeton Hall
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8dad66aafa6100295ef816
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
