BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Connection-Oriented Leader ft. Jody Wilson-Raybould and Roshan
  Danesh
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T200000
UID:6ac6a131ebad22004543c8a1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Spend an evening rethinking what leadership looks like when it
 's built on connection instead of hierarchy.\nMake the most of your Connec
 t IRL experience by coming before or staying after the featured event. Gra
 b a drink or snack at the bar. Try out tonight's\nConversation Station: Th
 e Common Ground\n\, where you can take a moment or two to connect with a s
 tranger around the topic of staying in relationship across difference.\nJo
 in us at the Toronto Reference Library's Appel Salon for\nThe Inbetween Le
 ader\n\, with Indigenous leader and former justice minister of Canada Jody
  Wilson-Raybould\, alongside internationally-renowned lawyer and peace-bui
 lding educator Roshan Danesh.\nTogether\, we'll take apart the assumption 
 that unity requires sameness\, examine what it means to stay connected to 
 people you don't always agree with\, and talk about why so many of our lea
 dership models are actually failing our need for connection. Expect to lea
 ve with a clearer sense of what it looks like to hold a room — or a work
 place\, or a community — together without flattening what makes it diver
 se. What does connection actually require of a leader\, beyond charisma or
  authority? How do Indigenous governance traditions offer a different mode
 l for staying in relationship through disagreement? And in a culture that 
 treats leadership as something you perform from the top\, what would it me
 an to reclaim it as something built collaboratively\, in the in-between sp
 aces of everyday interaction? Wilson-Raybould and Danesh\, drawing on Indi
 genous governance traditions and integrative worldviews in\nThe Inbetween 
 Leader: Cultivating Courage and Unity in a Complex World\n\, offer an alte
 rnative to competition-based leadership models — one that fosters interc
 onnectedness\, empowers diverse teams\, and maintains unity without erasin
 g difference. Building on their national bestsellers\nTrue Reconciliation\
 nand\nReconciling History\n\, they show how leaders in workplaces\, commun
 ities\, and families can create environments where everyone feels valued a
 nd genuinely connected. Come for a thoughtful conversation about the relat
 ionships that make real leadership possible.\nQ&A and book signing to foll
 ow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nJody Wils
 on-Raybould\nRoshan Danesh\nFarah Nasser\nRead more:\nThe Inbetween Leader
 \nIndian in the Cabinet\nReconciling History\n***\nConversation Station | 
 6:00–7:00 pm & 8–9 pm\nThe Common Ground\nA conversation station explo
 ring the relationships that keep us connected even when we disagree: When 
 has staying in a relationship with someone been harder than walking away 
 — and what made it worth it? Who has shown you what it looks like to lea
 d with humility? What's one thing you wish more people understood about co
 nnecting across difference? This station invites you to swap stories with 
 the strangers next to you about the everyday work of staying together.\nCo
 nnect IRL\nis not your average series of events\, workshops and activities
 \, but a real chance to connect with other Torontonians in meaningful and 
 sometimes unexpected ways. Through conversation stations meant to spark on
 e-on-one conversations\, or hands-on engagement activities and workshops b
 efore the featured event\, participants can explore the value of connectio
 n and community.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel S
 alon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDo
 ors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration requir
 ed.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest 
 number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6
 :45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBa
 r:\nBeer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\n***\nThis event
  is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and intern
 ational authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new boo
 ks and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal infor
 mation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the P
 ublic Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information 
 will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evalua
 tion. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following th
 e program. Questions about how the library handles your information can be
  directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yo
 nge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac6a131ebad22004543c8a1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
