BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bridging Minds: The Early Years (0 to 5): Small Actions\, Big Impa
 ct
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261118T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261118T190000
UID:6ad92376035faf0033de27c8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Dr Aliza Israel and Dr Tatiana Valverde\, child psychiatrists 
 at Women’s College Hospital\, will explore why the early years of life a
 re so important for children's mental health\, emotional well-being\, and 
 development. From birth to age five\, children begin to develop the founda
 tions for relationships\, emotional regulation\, learning\, and resilience
 . Through everyday interactions\, play\, and nurturing relationships\, par
 ents\, caregivers\, and communities can help create a strong foundation fo
 r lifelong well-being.\nWhat to Expect:\nUnderstanding Early      Childhoo
 d:\nWhy the first five years are an important period for brain      develo
 pment\, emotional growth\, and lifelong mental health\nThe Power of      R
 elationships:\nHow responsive caregiving\, secure relationships\,      pla
 y\, and everyday interactions help young children feel safe\, connected\, 
      and supported\nSupporting Big      Feelings:\nUnderstanding co-regula
 tion and how parents and caregivers can help      young children navigate 
 big emotions\, manage frustration\, and gradually      develop their own e
 motional regulation skills\nSmall Actions\,      Lasting Impact:\nPractica
 l\, everyday ways families\, caregivers\,      and communities can support
  children's mental health and help give every      child a healthy start\n
 About the Presenters:\nDr. Aliza Israel is a child and adolescent psychiat
 rist at Women's College Hospital\, and lecturer at the University of Toron
 to in child psychiatry and women's mental health. Her work focuses on the 
 interplay between maternal and child mental health\, and in promoting resi
 lience in young families. Dr. Israel lives in Toronto\, Canada\, with her 
 husband and three rapidly-growing boys.  She is also an amateur juggler wh
 o may or may not secretly wish to run away with the circus.\nDr. Tatiana V
 alverde da Conceicao is a Child and Adolescent Psychiatrist at the Reprodu
 ctive Life Stages Program at Women’s College Hospital. She’s an Assist
 ant Professor at University of Toronto. Her clinical work involves general
  Child and Adolescent Psychiatry with a special focus on Early Childhood M
 ental Health.  In addition to her clinical and academic work\, the experie
 nce of being a mother influences and enriches her professional perspective
 .\nAbout the Series\nBridging Minds: Health & Science Conversations is a p
 artnership between the Toronto Public Library and Women’s College Hospit
 al. Through monthly discussions led by experts from across different clini
 cal areas and scientific disciplines\, this series provides attendees with
  the opportunity to engage directly with our topics and speakers and empow
 ers communities to navigate health and science topics confidently\, inspir
 ing a future of informed decision-making.\nLearn more:\nhttps://www.womens
 collegehospital.ca/bridging-minds/\nReserve Your Spot Today!\nThis virtual
  Zoom event is free to join but registration is required. Once registered\
 , you’ll receive an email with the Zoom link.\nWe can’t wait to see yo
 u there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad92376035faf0033de27c8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
