BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:CANCELLED: Orhan Pamuk's The Museum of Innocence: Novel and Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T200000
UID:6a85b6e72cbfe80d71a4733d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:IMPORTANT UPDATE:\nDue to unforeseen circumstances\, this even
 t has been cancelled. We apologize for any inconvenience and look forward 
 to welcoming you to another event soon.\nNobel Prize-winner Orhan Pamuk sp
 eaks about the creation of Istanbul's Museum of Innocence.\nRenowned Turki
 sh novelist and Nobel Prize-winner Orhan Pamuk joins celebrated museum and
  cultural planning leader\, Gail Lord\, for an extraordinary conversation 
 about one of the most singular acts of imagination in contemporary literat
 ure and museology: the creation of Istanbul's Museum of Innocence.\nConcei
 ved in tandem with his acclaimed novel of the same name\, Pamuk's museum h
 ouses the fictional world of his characters Kemal and Füsun in a real\, p
 hysical space — a small house in Istanbul filled with everyday objects\,
  each one tied to a moment in the novel. The result is a haunting blur bet
 ween fiction and reality\, where a love story lives on not only on the pag
 e\, but in glass display cases\, drawers\, and rooms visitors can walk thr
 ough.\nHow does one curate a museum for people who never existed? What doe
 s it mean to give physical form to memory\, longing\, and melancholy — t
 he very textures of a novel? And what challenges arise when an author beco
 mes a museum-maker\, translating narrative into space\, object\, and atmos
 phere?\nOrhan Pamuk sits down with Gail Lord\, co-founder of Lord Cultural
  Resources and one of the world's leading museum planners\, to discuss the
  twin creation of the novel and the museum\, the ethereal act of turning f
 iction into physical experience\, and what he hoped visitors would feel wa
 ndering through the rooms of a story. The conversation will also turn to P
 amuk's evolving philosophy of museums — the ideas first laid out in his 
 "Modest Manifesto for Museums" — exploring his belief in small\, intimat
 e institutions that honor individual and everyday stories over the grand\,
  monumental narratives so often privileged by national museums.\nThe discu
 ssion will also look ahead to Pamuk's second museum project\, currently in
  development\, in which he continues to explore the relationship between s
 torytelling\, memory\, and collected objects. Building on the foundations 
 laid by the Museum of Innocence\, this new endeavor promises to further te
 st the boundaries between literature and lived space\, offering fresh insi
 ght into how narrative can be translated into an immersive\, tangible expe
 rience.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAb
 out this event’s guests:\nOrhan Pamuk\nGail Lord\nRead more:\nThe Museum
  of Innocence\nThe Innocence of Objects\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\
 nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)
 \, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nF
 ree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to en
 sure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tic
 kets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first c
 ome\, first served.\nBar:\nBeer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit 
 only).\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where w
 e host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversat
 ion about their new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Noti
 ce:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Pub
 lic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 
 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registratio
 n\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a br
 ief survey following the program. Questions about how the library handles 
 your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\
 , 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b6e72cbfe80d71a4733d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Between the Lines Bookclub
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T200000
UID:6962837f91e4576f847bd105@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Due to renovations the Bookclub will take place in Scadding Co
 urt Community Center in Room 4 (same building)\, participants will be resp
 onsible in sourcing copies of the monthly titles themselves as the branch
  will not be open to the public during this time. We apologize for the inc
 onvenience!\nBecome a page-turner by joining the Between the Lines Book Cl
 ub! Here\, we embark on a new chapter with every read\, diving into a worl
 d of endless possibilities. From gripping thrillers to heartwarming coming
 -of-age tales\, or a sprinkle of fantasy adventures\, there's something fo
 r everyone. Whether you're a die-hard fan of one genre or love mixing thin
 gs up\, you'll find a place here to discuss\, share\, and connect with fel
 low readers. Ready to explore different worlds and discover your next favo
 rite book? Join us and let's turn the page together!\nFriday November 16: 
 A Gentleman in Moscow by Amor Towles\nSet in Moscow in 1922\,\nA Gentlem
 an in Moscow\nby Amor Towles follows Count Alexander Rostov\, a refined Ru
 ssian aristocrat sentenced by a Bolshevik tribunal to house arrest in the 
 grand Metropol Hotel\, where he must spend the rest of his life confined w
 ithin its walls. As the world outside undergoes dramatic political and soc
 ial change\, the Count gradually transforms his restricted circumstances i
 nto a life rich with unexpected friendships\, meaningful encounters\, and 
 quiet adventures. From navigating the hotel's intricate social world to fo
 rming a particularly meaningful bond with a young girl named Nina\, Rostov
  discovers new perspectives on love\, loyalty\, personal growth\, and the 
 passage of time. Elegant\, witty\, and deeply moving\, the novel explores 
 the nature of freedom\, the importance of human connection\, and the possi
 bility of finding purpose and beauty even when life takes an unexpected tu
 rn.
LOCATION:Sanderson - Closed\, Main Floor Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6962837f91e4576f847bd105
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
