BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Go-Between Girl: Author Andrea Gunraj on Identity\, Memory\, and t
 he Indo-Caribbean Woman Experience in Canada
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261121T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261121T150000
UID:6ad796bae06996003a84bf4a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Meet\nAndrea Gunraj\n\, acclaimed essayist and author of\nGo
 -between Girl: My Indentured Roots as Reclaimed Present\n\, The Lost Siste
 r and The Sudden Disappearance of Seetha. Based out of Toronto\, Gunraj wr
 ites about underseen stories and connections. She will discuss her latest 
 work\, Go-Between Girl\, through storytelling and personal reflection\, ex
 ploring themes of family\, migration\, belonging\, and the complex interse
 ctions of Caribbean\, Indian\, and Canadian identities.\nGunraj will be in
  conversation with\nVictoria Kuketz\n\, a public policy leader and journal
 ist (IG @victoriakuketz). The discussion will be followed by an audience Q
 &A and book signing.\nPresented by the\nCaribbean Canadian Indian Cultural
  Collective (CCICC)\nand\nIndo Caribbean Educators Network (ICEN)\n\, this
  event offers a unique opportunity to engage with one of the most compelli
 ng voices in contemporary Indo-Caribbean literature. Attendees will gain i
 nsight into Gunraj's personal journey as a professional civic leader and I
 ndo-Caribbean woman\, and how historical and cultural experiences have sha
 ped Indo-Caribbean people. Discover the power of storytelling as memory\, 
 community building\, and cross-cultural understanding.\nWhether you are fa
 miliar with Andrea's work or discovering it for the first time\, this even
 t promises an inspiring and thought-provoking evening celebrating literatu
 re\, heritage\, and the diverse narratives that enrich Canada's multicultu
 ral landscape.\nFree program. Please register. Doors open at 12:45 pm. At
  1:00 pm\, seating becomes first-come\, first-served until capacity is rea
 ched.\nOrganization Info:\nThe\nCanadian Caribbean Indian Cultural Collect
 ive (CCICC)\nis a community organization representing Indians (currently r
 eferred to as South Asians) of various ethnicities\, regions\, and faiths\
 , who are the descendants of the indentured labourers taken by the British
  to their colonies in the Caribbean and around the world. Their mission is
  to share their stories of struggle\, resilience and contributions across 
 the Caribbean and here in Canada.\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto Pub
 lic Library:\nGo-between Girl: My Indentured Roots as Reclaimed Present\n(
 book)\nGo-Between Girl\n(ebook)\nGo-Between Girl\n(e-audiobook)\nThe Lost 
 Sister\n(book)\nOther\nbooks and formats available by Andrea Gunraj via T
 PL\n.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this fo
 rm is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries A
 ct\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used
  for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions a
 bout how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerlin
 e staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toront
 o ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Theatre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad796bae06996003a84bf4a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
