BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Myth of “Samshu”: Smell\, Race\, and the Making of Chinato
 wn in North America\, 1880s–1920s
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261127T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261127T200000
UID:6ad1264696ec3a003adad3f0@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What did Chinatown smell like to outsiders a century ago? How 
 could an unfamiliar distilled drink\nbecome a symbol of racial and cultura
 l difference? Between the 1880s and the 1920s\, Chinese\nimmigrant communi
 ties in North America encountered growing scrutiny of their businesses\,\n
 customs\, and everyday lives. Among the objects attracting attention was 
 “samshu\,” a term found in\nEnglish-language descriptions of Chinese d
 istilled spirits. Its reputation became entangled with\naccounts of Chinat
 own\, unfamiliar odours\, drinking practices\, illicit alcohol\, and anxie
 ties about\nimmigration.\nThis lecture investigates how descriptions of sa
 mshu circulated within the cultural imagination of\nNorth American society
 . Moving among sensory impressions\, racialized representations\, and the\
 nhistory of bootlegging and alcohol regulation\, it asks how ordinary expe
 riences of smell and taste\ncould become markers of inclusion and exclusio
 n. The lecture also asks audiences to distinguish\nclaims made about Chine
 se communities from the more complicated realities of their lives. In doin
 g\nso\, it explores how historical encounters produce durable ideas about 
 “us” and “them.”\nPresenter: Dr. Jackson Yue Bin Guo | Sessional L
 ecturer II\, Global Asia Studies / History\,\nUniversity of Toronto Scarbo
 rough | Founder\, Aprilum Lyceum\nJackson Yue Bin Guo received his Ph.D. i
 n History from the University of Toronto in 2023. He\nresearches food and 
 drink\, law\, sensory experience\, material culture\, and everyday life in
  late\nimperial and modern China. His current book manuscript\, Governing 
 Intoxication: Alcohol and the\nConvergence of Sensory Life in Late Imperia
 l and Modern China\, explores the social\, cultural\, and\nregulatory hist
 ories of distilled alcohol. His scholarship has appeared in Global Food Hi
 story and\nthe Asian Journal of Law and Society. He is the founder of Apri
 lum Lyceum.\nNo registration required.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ad1264696ec3a003adad3f0
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
