BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tiny Titans: Insects Up Close | Book Launch & Panel Talk with Auth
 or & Photographer Dara Ojo
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261202T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261202T200000
UID:6ac7e7bbea50a10050a6a31f@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Discover the hidden world of arthropods through stunning macro
  photography — a visual journey celebrating their beauty\, complexity\, 
 and essential role in our ecosystems.\nMeet\nDara Ojo\n\, an award-winning
  macro photographer and conservation storyteller whose work brings the hid
 den world of arthropods into vivid relief. Ojo's book\,\nTiny Titans : The
  Silent Keepers of Our Ecosystem\nreveals the fascinating lives of insects
 \, spiders\, and other small creatures that quietly sustain life on Earth.
  Captured across Canada\, Mexico\, Costa Rica\, and beyond\, Dara Ojo’s 
 photography highlights the intricate details and diversity of these often-
 overlooked species.\nOjo will be leading the conversation with:\nDr. Sand
 ra Rehan\n\, professor at York University and principal investigator of th
 e Rehan Lab with a focus on wild bees\, pollinator biology and conserva
 tion. Rehan's research explores the biology\, behaviour and evolution of b
 ees\, with a particular interest in understanding and protecting native po
 llinators.\nDr. Laura Timms\n\, Senior Specialist of Natural Heritage Man
 agement at Credit Valley Conservation and Adjunct Professor with the Dan
 iels Faculty’s Institute of Forestry and Conservation.\nLorraine Johnson
 \n\, author and native plant and ecological gardening advocate\, bridging 
 the connection between insects\, plants\, habitat and practical conservati
 on action.\nJoin in this evening to celebrate macro photography and the in
 tricate beauty and ecological importance of insects and spiders\, creature
 s that quietly anchor global biodiversity\, pollination\, and climate resi
 lience.\nFree. Register for a reminder.\n5:30 pm:\nDoors open. Mingle and 
 chat with fellow nature and environmental enthusiasts!\n6:00 pm:\nPresent
 ation begins. Enjoy a highly visual presentation combining photography\, 
 science\, conservation and storytelling\, taking the audience behind the s
 cenes of six years documenting tiny worlds across five continents and expl
 oring why these overlooked species matter. A light\, informal conversati
 on with guest speakers will explore why arthropods are so essential to ou
 r ecosystems\, with a discussion of challenges impacting local species\, a
 nd how science\, photography and art can work together to help people noti
 ce\, understand and care about the natural world. An audience Q&A follows 
 this discussion.\n7:30 pm:\nBook signing with books available for purchase
 .\n***\nThis program is part of TPL's\nOur Fragile Planet\nprogram series\
 , supported by Friends of Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.​\nQues
 tions? Please contact North York Central Library - Business\, Science & Te
 chnology Department.\n***\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Libr
 ary:\nTiny Titans : The Silent Keepers of Our Ecosystem\n(book) - Dara Ojo
 \nA Garden for the Rusty-patched Bumblebee\n(book) - Lorraine Johnson\n***
 \nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is
  collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, 
 sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for 
 event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about 
 how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline sta
 ff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W
  2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101 - 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac7e7bbea50a10050a6a31f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
