BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sven Beckert: A Global History of Capitalism
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261215T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261215T203000
UID:6acfd30896ec3a003ada9007@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL and Toronto Metropolitan University present historian Sven
  Beckert in conversation with Sanjay Ruparelia at the Toronto Reference Li
 brary's Appel Salon.\nAs the Laird Bell Professor of History at Harvard Un
 iversity and co-chair of the university's Program on the Study of Capitali
 sm\, Sven Beckert has spent his career tracing how a single economic syste
 m came to structure nearly every aspect of modern life\, how we work\, how
  we think about ourselves and others\, and how we organize our politics.\n
 In\nCapitalism: A Global History\n\, Beckert reveals that capitalism was b
 orn global\, corresponding at no point to an idealized dream of free marke
 ts\; from the outset\, state-backed institutions\, imperial expansion\, an
 d coercion shaped its dynamics as much as innovation and exchange did. Alo
 ng the way\, capitalism has both expanded political voice for some and vio
 lently foreclosed it for others\, entangled from its origins with slavery\
 , colonialism\, and profound inequality.\nHow do we distinguish between ca
 pitalism's capacity to generate genuine prosperity and its tendency to con
 centrate power in ways that undermine democratic institutions? What can ne
 arly a thousand years of capitalist history teach us about the conditions 
 under which economic systems support\, rather than erode\, democratic life
 ? And as we confront what many call a crisis of late-stage capitalism\, wh
 at possibilities remain for reimagining an economic order more compatible 
 with democratic values?\nJoin one of the world's foremost historians of ca
 pitalism for an essential conversation about the deep entanglement of mark
 ets and democracy\, and what history suggests about the road ahead.\nQuest
 ion and answer session and book signing to follow. Books available for pur
 chase.\nAbout this Event's Guests:\nSven Beckert\nSanjay Ruparelia\nRead M
 ore:\nCapitalism\nEmpire of Cotton\n***\nThis event is part of our\nOn Civ
 il Society\nseries\, where we host local and international authors\, activ
 ists and academics in conversation about the most critical topics of our t
 ime. Generously supported by the McConnell Foundation and also by Chris M.
  Reid.\n***\nAbout the series:\nOn the Frontlines of Democracy\nis a publi
 c lecture series to analyze the trajectory of democracy in the twenty-firs
 t century.\nIn every region\, the prospects of democracy face serious chal
 lenges\, old and new. Can we protect constitutional rights and renew colle
 ctive self-rule in an era of popular mistrust\, severe partisanship and re
 surgent nationalism? Can they reduce inequalities of power\, wealth and st
 atus\, defend deep diversity and confront climate change in the new digita
 l age? Can we develop innovative strategies to revitalize civic engagement
 \, empower public institutions and resist autocratic threats? And what can
  we offer\, learn and do to promote the prospects of democracy in our incr
 easingly post-western global order?\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT\n:\nLocation: Br
 am and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 
 2nd floor\nTime: Doors open 6:00 pm\, Event starts 7 pm.\nTickets: Free re
 gistration required.\nTicket policy: Tickets guaranteed until 6:50 pm\, af
 ter which rush line admission begins. Seating first come\, first served.\n
 Bar: Beer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\nTPL is committ
 ed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like t
 o request accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices
 @tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInf
 ormation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is coll
 ected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, secti
 ons 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event
  and program registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be c
 ontacted to fill out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions abo
 ut how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline 
 staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON 
 M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6acfd30896ec3a003ada9007
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Movie Screening: Home Alone
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261216T150000
UID:697104d02c65973d00191244@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:When bratty 8-year-old Kevin McCallister (Macaulay Culkin) act
 s out the night before a family trip to Paris\, his mother (Catherine O'Ha
 ra) makes him sleep in the attic. After the McCallisters mistakenly leave 
 for the airport without Kevin\, he awakens to an empty house and assumes h
 is wish to have no family has come true. But his excitement sours when he 
 realizes that two con men (Joe Pesci\, Daniel Stern) plan to rob the McCal
 lister residence\, and that he alone must protect the family home.\nRated 
 PG\n1 Hour 43 Minutes
LOCATION:Cedarbrae - Closed\, FCCR (First-Floor Auditorium)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/697104d02c65973d00191244
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
