BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Internet Horror Classics on Film: Backrooms
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261218T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261218T130000
UID:6ac6463ea85a2c00337dc4f1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In this final instalment of Toronto Public Library's virtual s
 eries Internet Horror Classics on Film\, actor Erin Mick and critic Michae
 l LaPointe join host Adam Nayman to discuss Kane Parsons' debut film\, Ba
 ckrooms\, which bends the liminal spaces that have haunted the internet fo
 r years into a mesmerizing horror film.\nAbout this event's guests:\nErin 
 Mick\nis an actor and writer based in Toronto. Her doctoral research cente
 rs on contemporary horror cinema\, with particular interest in emergent mo
 des of fan/viewer engagement\, as well as horror's relationship to and mob
 ilization of notions of trauma and collective memory. Erin also works as a
 n actor\, and is a writer of horror fiction.\nMichael LaPointe\nis a novel
 ist\, critic\, journalist and producer in Toronto\, Canada. He has publish
 ed a novel with Random House Canada\, written for The New Yorker\, The Atl
 antic\, and The Paris Review\, and produced series for BBC Radio 4. He is 
 a regular contributor to the Times Literary Supplement. His second novel\,
  Ruby Red\, will be released in spring 2027.\nAdam Nayman\nis a critic\, 
 teacher\, and author\, whose books include critical surveys of the Coen Br
 others\, Paul Thomas Anderson\, and most recently\, in Mind Games\, the fi
 lmmaker David Fincher. He is a regular contributor to The Ringer\, a lectu
 rer at the University of Toronto's Cinema Studies Institute\, and he has w
 ritten on film for The New Yorker and The New York Times.\nRead more:\nThe
  Creep\nImportant information for accessing this live virtual event:\nYou 
 must register for a free ticket via Eventbrite to gain access to this even
 t.\nThis event will be presented in a Zoom Webinar. Once registered\, you 
 will receive reminder emails from both Zoom and Eventbrite with links and 
 instructions to access your unique Zoom Webinar link to watch this event.\
 nAt the time of the event\, you must be signed in to Zoom or Eventbrite wi
 th the same email address that you used to book your ticket in order to ac
 cess your unique event link. Unique event links can only be open on one de
 vice at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at Toronto Public Library\nWe are comm
 itted to making our content accessible to as many Torontonians as possible
 . Please call or email us if you are Deaf or have a disability and would l
 ike to request accommodation to participate in this program. Please let us
  know as far in advance as possible and we will do our best to meet your r
 equest. At least three weeks' notice is preferred. Phone 416-393-7099 or e
 mail accessibleservices@tpl.ca\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe p
 ersonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Librar
 y under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The
  information will only be used for event and program registration\, delive
 ry and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contacted to fill out a brief surve
 y following the program\n. Questions about how the library handles your in
 formation can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-3
 97-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac6463ea85a2c00337dc4f1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
